A||N||3||Cplensdorf||Paint Refinishing||||1||
Z||000000||Cplensdorf||03-18-2001||10:43 PM||||I have got an 88GT that has original black paint and is for the most part in great shape.  It is showing the typical clear coat cracking/peeling on the top of the two front fenders.  I would like to retain the original paint, but must do something about the fenders.  Has anyone solved this problem without a repaint?  If so, what was the process for smoothing out the clear coat/refinishing?<P>I did see an article/post last week on another Fiero site regarding a process of sanding and applying 3M products.  Any reference to this article would be great as I can no longer find it.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||09:32 AM||||you should have no problems at all painting just the front fenders. Black will always match fine. Any basecoat/clearcoat will work. Just pick a brand and whether you want or can find laquer, or urathane. You will have to do the black base coat too. A pint of each will be plenty. Make sure you get all the peeling clear off or the new coat will come off with it too. Sometimes you can take like 2 or 3 inch tape and stick down hard and pull it off to the basecoat, or use a single edge razor blade to slice the bad clear off. Ive peeled off 3/4 of a whole car like that in a half a day.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||03-19-2001||10:40 AM||||If you cant re-paint the fenders, you can always sand/buff.  I had some of the clear wearing away on my left front fender, so there was a little ridge there.  I wet sanded with 800 to knock the ridge down, and then used 1500 grit to feather edge it.  Used Meguiar's Diamond Cut buffing compound and then the Dual Action Cleaner/Polish of the same brand.  It came out great.<P>But, I am repainting the whole car soon (when it gets warm), so it was kinda a temporary fix for me.<P><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||11:54 AM||||yea joe. that is only a very temp fix. the basecoat (blk) is natually flat. buffing will give it some shine, but itll go away pretty quick. Basecoats must have clear topcoat.||||reg||1
